## ===========================================================================
## JAIL AND THE SEWERS — Book 6 (`story.ni:7597-8545`) and Book 7
## (`story.ni:8546-8742`), Chapter 5.
##
## The change document ("Chapter 5 — Jail and the Sewers", 2026-09-01)
## authorizes both books together, from the sack coming off in the cell to
## daylight on Commerce Street: the waking and the confiscation, Bobby's
## voice through the wall, Jacobs and the wire, the lock picked through the
## window, the jailhouse and its three doors, Jacobs's offer, Olmer and
## Darrens, the north door, the drain room and the grate, the drop, the
## sewer and its painted glyphs, the ladder and the morning. Every line is
## Gentry's; conversations are STUBS under the standing rule.
##
## THE RULINGS (change document, Chapter 5):
##   * PRESSURE — "the guards come back", with teeth. A clock on the player
##     (`guards-return`, forty turns) starts as Jacobs stirs (JA2, the turn
##     after BO22) and stops when she drops into the sewer; if it runs out
##     while she is anywhere in the jail — the drain room included — it is
##     the capture ending (JA22's room: a dozen Lord's Guard and Rudup), as
##     ONE placeholder death text. MEASURED AND REPORTED, not decided: the
##     shortest path from JA2 to the drop is twelve turns (talk, three at
##     the lock, the straw, the door, out, the wire, the north door, the
##     alcove, the grate, the hole); forty is three times that. Probes that
##     poked at everything on the way died at twenty and at thirty — the
##     number is David's to tune. A one-in-six warning slot rides the clock
##     (`meanwhile`) — a placeholder for whatever warns her, if anything.
##   * WHO SEES HER — Bobby and Jacobs do not ("Jack", "kid", "runt"
##     carry); Rudup and OLMER see — every "girl", "miss", "young lady" of
##     Olmer's carries as a tell, verbatim.
##
## STUBS TAKING THE SOURCE'S PATH (flagged, per the standing rule's item 4):
## Bobby's voice runs on a timer as the source's beats do (BO16, then BO18,
## BO20, BO22 one per turn), each a TODO; Jacobs's JA2 fires a turn after
## BO22; `talk to jacobs` then prints the JA3-JA7 TODO and starts his
## attempt at the lock (three turns, the muttering on the second) which
## ends with JA8's consequence — the wire in the straw; in the jailhouse
## JA16 fires on both being there, JA19 two turns on (Olmer's interjection
## inside it), OL1 a turn after that, JA21 two turns after JA19 or on `talk
## to` (the stub takes JA20 "No, thanks" — JA22, going with him, is not
## reachable through a stub; its death text is kept in a comment below for
## the rewrite) or when she starts on the north door with him there; Olmer's
## OL9/OL10 TODOs name the two of them (OL5's side effect) but do NOT unlock
## the north door for her — OL4's side effect is the player's own `unlock`,
## which the clock makes a real choice.
##
## THE SEWER PUZZLE. The source shuffles four colours and eight symbols over
## four glyphs after every move and keeps one "magic" colour; Chord draws
## nothing but chances and moves nobody but the player, so: the magic colour
## is drawn once at the drop (four states on `the magic color`), and the
## glyph layout is one of FOUR ROTATIONS of a fixed colour cycle (`the
## glyphs`: layout-a puts red north, blue east, green south, yellow west;
## each later layout turns it a quarter clockwise), redrawn on each correct
## move. The marked tunnel is where the magic colour sits. Symbols ride the
## colours (red square, blue diamond, green circle, yellow triangle). Exits
## cannot change target at runtime and `is deadly while` is not wired, so
## the two moves are geometry: the Sewer's four exits lead to four TUNNEL
## rooms (the first move), whose four exits lead to four BEND rooms (the
## second); each arrival checks its own direction against the marked one —
## wrong is the source's death text, right redraws the glyphs (first move)
## or reaches the Access Tunnel (second). The rooms all carry the sewer's
## text; the titles name the tunnel taken. WITHOUT OLMER at the drop the
## source draws nothing — the defaults leave every glyph red and the magic
## glyph the north one, so north twice wins by accident. Carried as the
## source has it: no draw without him, `layout-a` and `red` stand, north
## twice. REPORTED (change document gap 3), not decided.
##
## WHAT SHE LOSES (gap 4, reported): the confiscation removes everything but
## the baggy clothes — in the port that is the old gray cloak, the woolen
## cap, the boots, the cloth satchel with whatever it holds (Teisha's dress
## and hat, the day's takings), and the apple or the necklace if she still
## has them in hand. Nothing returns; nothing later needs them.
##
## NAMES. Chord cannot rename an entity, so "hulking shape" is Jacobs from
## the first turn (aliases carry the source's words), and the platform's
## "You can see Jacobs here." names him before JA2 does; the bald-headed man
## and the skinny man are Olmer and Darrens the same way. The source's
## initial-appearance lines ride the rooms as gated markers (as Chapter 4).
##
## Not carried, noted: the cell doors' `tmpdirection` name games (three
## named doors instead); `look through <door>` from the jailhouse lists the
## occupants by state rather than by counting; `talk to bobby` through the
## wall (Bobby is offstage — the beats are timed); JA22's death (no stub
## reaches it; text kept below); `follow <glyph>` (a topic slot cannot name
## a glyph); the exits report in the drain room; `xyzzy`; `kiss`; the
## north door stays shut after the drunks leave it (nobody goes back in).
## Glue text of mine is marked GLUE.
## ===========================================================================

