## GLUE — the door answering the drunks' names once they have gone.

define phrase no-one-in-that-cell
  There's no one in that cell now.
end phrase

define phrase peer-out-at-jailhouse
  You peer through the closely spaced bars. You can see the jailhouse main
  room on the other side.
end phrase

define phrase peer-in-at-jacobs
  You peer through the closely spaced bars. You can see a cramped, filthy
  cell on the other side. Jacobs is inside.
end phrase

define phrase peer-in-at-two-men
  You peer through the closely spaced bars. You can see a cramped, filthy
  cell on the other side. A bald-headed man and a skinny man are inside.
end phrase

define phrase peer-in-at-olmer-and-darrens
  You peer through the closely spaced bars. You can see a cramped, filthy
  cell on the other side. Olmer and Darrens are inside.
end phrase

define phrase peer-in-at-empty-cell
  You peer through the closely spaced bars. You can see a cramped, filthy
  cell on the other side.
end phrase

define phrase jacobs-blocks-the-view
  You can't; Jacobs is blocking your view.
end phrase

## `story.ni:8276`, verbatim.

define phrase lock-picked-through-the-window
  You slip your arm through the bars and reach down for the lock. It's a
  stretch — you have to get up on your tip-toes and strain your shoulder
  painfully against the little window — but soon you feel the metal of the
  lockplate, and your fingers find the tiny, sharp-edged hole in the middle.
  You jam the end of the lockpick in and start wiggling it around. Almost
  immediately, you realize that this is not going to work. The angle is too
  awkward, and you can't see what you're doing, and you can't feel the
  tumblers the way Bobby showed you when you practiced on the front door of
  Maiden House...

  Then you feel the wire bump against something inside the lock. You manage
  to lever the end of the wire under something and push... you feel it start
  to give... you push a little harder, and it starts to pivot... you push
  harder, and it starts to slip off the end of your pick...

  And then there is a snap, and the pick jumps out of your fingers and
  tumbles to the ground outside the cell door.

  Jacobs suddenly stands up. He walks over, puts his hand on the door... and
  pushes it open.

  'Well,' he says, looking you over with a sly expression, 'you are a
  valuable runt, after all.'
end phrase

## `story.ni:8424`, verbatim, the named and unnamed halves.

define phrase north-door-picked
  Picking a lock is much easier when you're standing on the ground and can
  see what you're doing. Twenty seconds, and you have the cell door open.
end phrase

define phrase olmer-thanks-nameless
  'Oh, thank you, miss, thank you kindly. Goddesses' blessings upon you.
  Allow me to make introductions,' the man wheezes. 'I am called Olmer, and
  this is my associate, Darrens.' The skinny man standing behind him smiles
  and nods enthusiastically.
end phrase

define phrase olmer-thanks-named
  'Oh, thank you, miss, thank you kindly. Goddesses' blessings upon you.'
end phrase

define phrase olmer-leads-southwest
  'Now,' says Olmer, 'if you will follow us, we will make our escape.'

  Olmer and Darrens go southwest.
end phrase

